WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.824 --> 00:00:16.870
My name is Delia, and I am a 37-year-old weaver from Chinchero, Peru.

2
00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:23.853
My village is high in the Andes, located over one thousand feet above the Urubamba River.

3
00:00:24.593 --> 00:00:31.016
The view is breathtaking, but what makes Chinchero unique is that here, even though it is now 2008,

4
00:00:32.288 --> 00:00:34.230
we are preserving ancient traditions.

5
00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:41.516
In fact, Chinchero is just outside the city of Cuzco, which was once the center of the mighty

6
00:00:41.636 --> 00:00:42.536
Inca Empire.

7
00:00:43.437 --> 00:00:50.383
As a traditional master weaver, I have the privilege and responsibility of teaching my skills to the younger

8
00:00:50.423 --> 00:00:53.745
generation, uniting the past with the future.

9
00:00:54.526 --> 00:00:59.810
In fact, I'm waiting for Carla, a neighbor's daughter, to come and take her weaving lesson.

10
00:01:00.451 --> 00:01:05.154
For many women like me, weaving is not just a craft but a way of life.

11
00:01:05.915 --> 00:01:13.500
Peruvian textiles have a rich history that can be traced as far back as 2500 BC, long before

12
00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:14.120
the Incas.

13
00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:21.725
By the time of the Inca Empire, in the 1500s, hand-weaving was widely practiced, even by men.

14
00:01:22.386 --> 00:01:29.590
The patterns woven into our textiles have always told stories, so our history was not just expressed orally

15
00:01:29.991 --> 00:01:31.792
but was also woven into cloth.

16
00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:42.143
More recently our weaving tradition was sadly being lost as many village people moved to cities, seeking money

17
00:01:42.303 --> 00:01:43.784
and a more modern way of life.

18
00:01:44.564 --> 00:01:50.387
But some, like me, have stayed in the village to keep our traditional culture alive.

19
00:01:50.547 --> 00:01:57.490
In 1996 a weaving center was established here, where women, young and old, can come to learn our

20
00:01:57.610 --> 00:01:58.830
unique weaving skills.

21
00:01:59.771 --> 00:02:05.877
It is called the Center for Traditional Textiles, and I started to work and teach here in 2000.

22
00:02:06.578 --> 00:02:13.020
The mission of the center is to celebrate the history and traditions of Peruvian textiles and to educate

23
00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:14.021
others about them.

24
00:02:14.801 --> 00:02:21.004
After the women weavers gain expertise, their work is sold at the center or at local markets.

25
00:02:21.764 --> 00:02:27.187
This allows the women to gain financial independence or contribute to their family's income.

26
00:02:28.127 --> 00:02:34.415
Practice is a must for good weavers, for we do not rely on fancy, high-speed machines to do

27
00:02:34.455 --> 00:02:35.276
the work for us.

28
00:02:36.177 --> 00:02:44.203
Our tools are our fingers—mostly our thumbs—and the yarn we weave comes from either alpaca or vicuna, animals

29
00:02:44.243 --> 00:02:45.724
that resemble small llamas.

30
00:02:46.625 --> 00:02:52.450
The alpaca are especially important to our way of life because in addition to their wool, they also

31
00:02:52.510 --> 00:02:55.032
supply meat, fertilizer, fuel, and leather.

32
00:02:56.013 --> 00:02:59.656
When I was a child, I often tended our sheep and alpaca.

33
00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:06.962
As I watched them graze, I practiced weaving jakima strips, small pieces that all the children in my

34
00:03:07.002 --> 00:03:09.843
village make to practice different textile patterns.

35
00:03:10.623 --> 00:03:17.105
This practice prepared me to learn more complicated patterns when I was seventeen and began my lessons with

36
00:03:17.165 --> 00:03:18.065
an older weaver.

37
00:03:19.126 --> 00:03:25.387
Alpaca and vicuna wool can be many shades of brown, cream, and gray, but we also dye the

38
00:03:25.407 --> 00:03:31.464
wool using natural sources, such as cochineal beetles, which live on prickly pear cactus.

39
00:03:32.224 --> 00:03:37.629
These beetles, when dried and crushed, yield a dye that makes our yarn a brilliant color of red.

40
00:03:38.330 --> 00:03:44.635
We also use eucalyptus leaves and beans to make dyes, and the vibrant colors from these plants range

41
00:03:44.675 --> 00:03:47.117
from pink to different shades of brown and green.

42
00:03:47.998 --> 00:03:52.842
How bright or deep the color is depends on the color of the plant and how long you

43
00:03:52.882 --> 00:03:54.424
leave the wool in the dye bath.

44
00:03:55.324 --> 00:04:00.207
Our weavers use a backstrap loom, which is very easy to use and carry around.

45
00:04:01.107 --> 00:04:04.309
Whenever I start a new textile, I make a new loom!

46
00:04:05.230 --> 00:04:10.873
First, I measure out the yarn for the warp, which consists of the threads that will run lengthwise

47
00:04:10.913 --> 00:04:11.833
in my textile.

48
00:04:12.594 --> 00:04:17.616
Then, I tie the warp to something immobile, such as a tree or a stake in the ground.

49
00:04:18.697 --> 00:04:25.248
For smaller textile pieces, some women use their own bodies by stretching out a leg and tying the

50
00:04:25.289 --> 00:04:26.249
warp to their foot.

51
00:04:27.110 --> 00:04:31.012
The other end of the warp has a strap that is tied around the weaver's waist.

52
00:04:31.752 --> 00:04:36.935
Then, I interlace threads of yarn horizontally across the warp to create the pattern.

53
00:04:37.776 --> 00:04:39.877
The horizontal threads are called the woof.

54
00:04:41.058 --> 00:04:45.581
With all the different colors of yarn, we can make lots of patterns in our textiles.

55
00:04:46.421 --> 00:04:53.029
For example, there is a very special Incan pattern called the eye, which can be combined with other

56
00:04:53.089 --> 00:04:59.570
patterns to represent many things, from the burning of the sun to a gentle whirlpool in a meandering

57
00:04:59.610 --> 00:05:08.693
river. Each textile tells a story about our mountains, rivers, animals, and villages—sometimes even about the weaver herself.

58
00:05:09.993 --> 00:05:17.096
Because patterns and colors are chosen by the weaver to make each textile distinctive, dark colors may mean

59
00:05:17.136 --> 00:05:22.838
the weaver is sad, and bright colors may reflect the happiness she felt as she was weaving.

60
00:05:23.739 --> 00:05:30.583
The most important family textile is the manta, a cape that has many functions— from hauling fruit and

61
00:05:30.643 --> 00:05:33.564
firewood to carrying babies on mothers' backs.

62
00:05:34.845 --> 00:05:40.968
Each village has its own unique manta pattern, so when others see you, they recognize what village you

63
00:05:41.029 --> 00:05:43.510
come from by the colorful design of your manta.

64
00:05:44.210 --> 00:05:49.626
When I weave outside, I sit on a manta, and whatever I am weaving, whether it is a

65
00:05:49.686 --> 00:05:54.189
belt, a hat, or a skirt, I put a lot of time and effort into each piece.

66
00:05:55.049 --> 00:06:00.473
Market day is special for the women of Chinchero, and we like to dress in our traditional handmade

67
00:06:00.533 --> 00:06:02.154
clothes when we go to the market.

68
00:06:03.315 --> 00:06:08.999
We wear colorful jackets, braid our hair, and wear our hats, called monteras, proudly.

69
00:06:09.539 --> 00:06:13.242
In our village everyone wears a montera with a round top.

70
00:06:14.122 --> 00:06:19.101
The brim of the hat curves toward its center, and inside the brim is a secret fold where

71
00:06:19.141 --> 00:06:20.341
we can store our money.

72
00:06:20.981 --> 00:06:26.623
When a tourist stops by and purchases one of my textiles, I just pop the money into my

73
00:06:26.663 --> 00:06:32.784
hat! The weaving center helps with the sale of our work, but it is up to each weaver

74
00:06:32.824 --> 00:06:34.125
to negotiate a price.

75
00:06:35.105 --> 00:06:40.206
To be honest, I don't make a lot of money, only about three hundred dollars a year.

76
00:06:40.847 --> 00:06:44.748
But I am satisfied when I see how my weaving has made someone happy.

77
00:06:44.768 --> 00:06:47.197
Oh, here is Carla!

78
00:06:47.778 --> 00:06:54.126
Someday she will make our village proud and weave beautiful textiles that will tell the story of her

79
00:06:54.227 --> 00:06:54.907
generation.
